CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE HARNESS_APP.ARCHIVE_OLD_ORDERS(
  p_order_date_before IN DATE
)
AS
  TYPE t_orders IS TABLE OF HARNESS_APP.ORDERS%ROWTYPE;
  l_orders t_orders;

  CURSOR c_old_orders IS
    SELECT *
    FROM HARNESS_APP.ORDERS
    WHERE ORDER_DATE < p_order_date_before;

BEGIN
  OPEN c_old_orders;

  LOOP
    FETCH c_old_orders BULK COLLECT INTO l_orders LIMIT 10000;

    EXIT WHEN l_orders.COUNT = 0;

    FORALL i IN INDICES OF l_orders
      INSERT INTO HARNESS_APP.ORDERS_ARCHIVE(
        ORDER_ID,
        CUSTOMER_ID,
        ORDER_DATE,
        STATUS,
        TOTAL
      )
      VALUES (
        l_orders(i).ORDER_ID,
        l_orders(i).CUSTOMER_ID,
        l_orders(i).ORDER_DATE,
        l_orders(i).STATUS,
        l_orders(i).TOTAL
      );

    FORALL i IN INDICES OF l_orders
      DELETE FROM HARNESS_APP.ORDERS
      WHERE ORDER_ID = l_orders(i).ORDER_ID;
  END LOOP;

  CLOSE c_old_orders;
END ARCHIVE_OLD_ORDERS;
/
```

Assumptions:

- “Older than a given date” is implemented as `ORDER_DATE < p_order_date_before`.
- `p_order_date_before` is a normal `DATE` value. If it is `NULL`, the predicate matches no rows and the procedure does nothing.
- The procedure does not `COMMIT` or `ROLLBACK`; the caller controls the transaction.
- The proposal is complete as written, but it has not been compiled here.
- No tie-breaking or ordering rule is needed for the archive/delete operation itself. The cursor processes rows in whatever order Oracle returns them; the procedure does not depend on a specific order.

